  "num_turns": null,
  "final_message": "I fixed the bug in `session_ttl.py`, and `python3 -m unittest -v test_public.py` now passes (1 test, OK).\n\n**The bug:** line 10 removed the timezone from `now` with `.replace(tzinfo=None)` but kept it on `created`. With timezone-aware inputs like `+00:00`, the subtraction then failed with `TypeError: can't subtract offset-naive and offset-aware datetimes`.\n\n**The fix:** I deleted the `.replace(tzinfo=None)`, so both timestamps keep whatever timezone they were given.\n\nTwo extra checks behaved correctly:\n- Different offsets are compared by actual time: `00:00+00:00` against `01:00:30+01:00` is 30 seconds apart, so a 60-second TTL is not expired.\n- Two timestamps without a timezone still work: 59 seconds against a 60-second TTL is not expired.\n\nMixing one timestamp with a timezone and one without still raises `TypeError`, which Python's datetime does anyway.\n\nThe linter says the file doesn't match the formatter's style, probably because of the long `ttl_seconds` check on line 7, which was already there. I left it to keep the fix minimal. `format_expiry_message` is unchanged.\n\nDONE: Removed the one-sided `.replace(tzinfo=None)` on `now` so timezone-aware timestamps subtract correctly; the public test passes.",
