      "final_message": "I rewrote `merge_intervals` in `solution.py` so it follows the README. Both public tests pass (`python3 -m unittest -v test_public.py`: 2 tests OK).\n\n- **Input checks:** a non-list `intervals` raises `TypeError`. An item raises `ValueError` if it doesn't have exactly two elements, has a non-int or bool value, or has start greater than end.\n- **Merging:** intervals are sorted by start. The next one is merged into the current one when it starts at or before the current end, so touching intervals like `[1,2]` and `[2,3]` become `[1,3]`.\n- **Output:** it always returns a new list of `[start, end]` lists. The input list and its items are never changed, and empty input returns `[]`.\n\nI also ran a few quick checks beyond the tests: unsorted mixed list/tuple input, touching intervals, empty input, each kind of bad item, and a tuple passed instead of a list. All behaved as the README says.\n\nDONE: Rewrote merge_intervals to validate input (TypeError/ValueError), sort and merge overlapping or touching intervals into a new list without mutating the input; public tests pass.",
